<p>Imagine a computer running somewhere. You connect over SSH with your key. A Unix environment appears in front of you, and <code>pwd</code> shows that you are in your own home directory. You type <code>who</code> and see other people logged into the same machine as you. You don't know who they are, but you can <code>finger</code> them and read their <code>.plan</code>. You can even run <code>write</code> to send them a message that will pop up in their terminal. Someone replies that there is an IRC server where everyone hangs out. You run <code>weechat</code> and join the conversation. They say hello. One user tells you there is more documentation on the "forum", a bulletin board, and that you can open it with <code>bbj</code>. You find plenty of information. Reading the threads, you discover that you have a personal website in <code>~/public_html</code>, published at <code>https://server/~user/</code>. You quickly run <code>nano ~/public_html/index.html</code> and write a few lines of HTML. You check how it looks with <code>lynx</code>. You send an internal email with <code>alpine</code> to the IRC contact who helped you earlier, thanking them and inviting them to visit your new site. While you wait for an answer, you open <code>tin</code>, the news reader (NNTP), to catch up on the news of your small community.</p>
<p>This is not nostalgia for the 90s: it exists today, and it is called a tilde.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--what-is-a-tilde" class="blog-single__content-title">What is a tilde</h2>
<p>A tilde is a shared, public access Unix computer. Its admin gives you an account, you log in over SSH and you get your own home directory, your shell, your website, your email and your programs. Resources are shared with the rest of the users: the CPU, the disk, the installed programs, and so on.</p>
<p>It is not a platform, a service or a protocol. It is a machine with Unix, an admin and a community.</p>
<p>Paul Ford, the creator of tilde.club, put it like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Tilde.club is one cheap, unmodified Unix computer on the Internet.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In practice, almost all of them share these features:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A shell account</strong>: you log in with <code>ssh user@server</code> and work in the terminal as if it were your own computer.</li>
<li><strong>A personal website</strong>: whatever you save in <code>~/public_html</code> is published at <code>https://server/~user/</code>.</li>
<li><strong>A local community</strong>: IRC chat, internal email, terminal forums and classic commands to see who is online.</li>
</ul>
<p>They are non-profit: they run on donations and on the time of one or more volunteer admins. And this works because they are small servers, from a dozen to a few thousand users.</p>
<h3 id="blog-single-content__title--why-it-is-called-a-tilde" class="blog-single__content-title">Why it is called a tilde</h3>
<p>Because of the <code>~</code> symbol. On Unix, the tilde stands for your home directory. As Paul Ford said, it is a little like the <code>@</code> on Twitter, a shortcut that says "here is a person".</p>
<p>Here is an anecdote you can tell your grandchildren. That use of the tilde comes from the Lear-Siegler ADM-3A terminal from the 1970s, where the <code>~</code> key and the <em>Home</em> key were the same.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--the-tildeverse" class="blog-single__content-title">The Tildeverse</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://tildeverse.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Tildeverse</a> describes itself as "a loose association of like-minded tilde communities". There is no central authority. Each server has its own admins and rules, but they share services and interests. One tilde may only offer Gemini and Gopher, while another has IRC, Mastodon and live radio. The key is that users can move between them and meet: it is a federation of servers, not of applications.</p>
<p>As I write these lines, it has 13 members: aussies.space, cosmic.voyage, ctrl-c.club, envs.net, rw.rs, texto-plano.xyz, tilde.club, tilde.green, tilde.guru, tilde.institute, tilde.pink, tilde.team and tilde.town. If you like to poke around, the full list is available as <a href="https://tildeverse.org/members.json" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">JSON</a>, with the creation date, operating system and admins of each one. There is a bit of everything: Debian, Fedora, OpenBSD, FreeBSD and even NetBSD.</p>
<p>On top of the servers there is a layer of shared services:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href="https://tilde.chat/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tilde.chat</a></strong>: the Tildeverse IRC network (<code>irc.tilde.chat</code>, port 6697 with TLS), with web chat, an XMPP gateway and a Mumble server.</li>
<li><strong><a href="https://tilde.zone/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tilde.zone</a></strong>: a Mastodon instance for tilde users.</li>
<li><strong><a href="https://tilde.news/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tilde.news</a></strong>: a link aggregator in the style of Hacker News.</li>
<li><strong><a href="https://tilderadio.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tilderadio.org</a></strong>: a radio station with live DJs. "It's radio minus radio."</li>
<li><strong><a href="https://tildegit.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tildegit.org</a></strong>: a Git forge for community projects.</li>
<li>Also: a terminal forum (bbj), a zine, a shared journal, a pastebin and Gopher space.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nobody forces you to use every service, to socialize or to explore the Tildeverse. But the fun is in bumping into people from your server or from others.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--house-rules" class="blog-single__content-title">House rules</h2>
<p>Each tilde has its own code of conduct, and almost all of them fit in one line. This is tilde.club's:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No drama. Be respectful. Have fun.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>tilde.town's is even shorter: "Be kind to each other". But behind the brevity there are clear rules:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No cryptocurrency mining</strong>: on tilde.town it means an immediate and permanent ban.</li>
<li><strong>Nothing commercial</strong>: your web space is not for business or advertising.</li>
<li><strong>Don't hog resources</strong>: the machine belongs to everyone. Don't use it as a backup drive, a proxy or a backend for your IDE.</li>
<li><strong>No port scanning and no VPNs</strong>: tilde.team bans them, and it has also added a very 2026 rule: no AI agents or services on the server.</li>
</ul>
<p>My favorite is the motto of texto-plano.xyz, the Spanish-speaking tilde: "let's be few but united". It sums up the philosophy better than any manifesto. A small community is not a limitation, it is the goal. That is why signup forms ask what interests you about the server: they want to make sure there is a curious human being on the other side.</p>
