</div><p><strong>SDF</strong> deserves a special mention. It is the grandfather of them all, with almost 40 years behind it, and it works with a different model: the basic account is free, and a one-time fee of $36 (ARPA) gives you lifetime access to more services.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--not-everything-is-pretty" class="blog-single__content-title">Not everything is pretty</h2>
<p>Tildes are machines with people behind them, and that comes with risks.</p>
<p>In May 2026, tilde.town had to close access because of a kernel vulnerability with no patch available. It opened, closed and reopened over two weeks, disabling kernel modules and replacing Nginx with Caddy.</p>
<p>In August it was tilde.team's turn. Someone got root access and ran <code>rm -rf /</code>. Daily backups limited the loss to a few hours, but they had to disable SSH and move the shared Tildeverse services to another machine.</p>
<p>Sharing a computer with thousands of strangers has a price:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>You depend on the admin</strong>. If they get tired, fall ill or lose interest, the server disappears. Grex closed in 2023 after 32 years, and the list of dead tildes is long.</li>
<li><strong>Root sees everything</strong>. Your files live on someone else's machine. Don't keep secrets there.</li>
<li><strong>Security is shared</strong>. A kernel bug or a compromised account affects the whole community.</li>
<li><strong>Your identity is borrowed</strong>. <code>~user@server</code> is yours as long as the server exists, just like an account on a Mastodon instance.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are risks you should know and accept before requesting your account.</p>
<h2 id="blog-single-content__title--final-notes" class="blog-single__content-title">Final notes</h2>
<p>Tildes are a corner that makes the internet a nicer place: you log in, say hello, see who is around, learn new commands, edit your website and read the news. A place shared with people as curious as you.</p>
<p>In 2014, Paul Ford wrote that when people talk about "cloud computing" they are talking about "millions of tiny ghost towns": servers running where nobody lives. A tilde is the exact opposite: a small town that smells of coffee, with neighbors who greet and help each other.</p>
<p>If you end up joining a tilde, I would love to <code>finger</code> you. ;)</p>
